<div class="tab-content active" id="notes-1-2">

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Positive vs Normative Economics</h2>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="content-card">
        <div class="content-card-title">Positive economics</div>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Objective, testable statements about what <em>is</em>. Positive statements describe or predict, and they can in principle be confirmed or refuted by evidence.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem; margin-top:0.5rem;">Example: "A fall in interest rates increases consumer spending."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <div class="content-card-title">Normative economics</div>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Value judgements about what <em>should</em> be. Normative statements reflect opinions and cannot be settled by evidence alone.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem; margin-top:0.5rem;">Example: "Lowering interest rates is the best way to help small businesses succeed."</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;">Can you tell the difference?</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Statement</th><th>Type</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>The unemployment rate in New Zealand is 5.2%.</td><td>Positive</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Increasing education funding will boost long-term economic growth.</td><td>Positive</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reducing income inequality makes society fairer.</td><td>Normative</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The average price of petrol rose by 5% last year.</td><td>Positive</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Banning single-use plastics is the right way to protect the environment.</td><td>Normative</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A cut in income tax will increase average household disposable income.</td><td>Positive</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Banning petrol motorbikes will affect low-income households most.</td><td>Positive</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Banning petrol motorbikes is unjust.</td><td>Normative</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="callout remember" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <div class="callout-title">Economics is Dangerous, Warning 1</div>
      <p>Positive statements sometimes sound like they carry an opinion, but they don't. The judgement comes from you. For example, "Going on holiday is inefficient" is a positive statement. Your brain might add "…and that's bad", but that addition is normative. Be alert to when you are adding a "good" or "bad" to something that is only describing what is.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout remember" style="margin-top:1rem;">
      <div class="callout-title">Economics is Dangerous, Warning 2</div>
      <p>Do not use economics to defend an argument that is not really an economic argument. If you rely on economics to support your argument, what will you do if the economics is disproven? Your real reason for believing it has not changed, but you have just lost the ground you chose to stand on. Argue what you actually mean, and bring the economics in as backup where it genuinely helps.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warning" style="margin-top:1rem;">
      <div class="callout-title">Case study: arguing against slavery with economics</div>
      <p>In 1857 Hinton Rowan Helper, a non-slaveholding white North Carolinian, published <em>The Impending Crisis of the South: How to Meet It</em>. It is an economics book rather than a moral tract, and that is what made it both effective and explosive. Using the 1850 census data, Helper argued that slavery was economically ruinous, not for enslaved people, but for the South's non-slaveholding white majority. The North had far outpaced the South in manufacturing, trade, transport, education and wealth per person, and he blamed the slave system directly: it concentrated land and capital in a small planter elite, crowded out free labour and small farmers, and left the South dependent on the North to process and resell its own raw goods at a markup. Southern states banned the book. The Republican party handed it out as campaign material.</p>
      <p>Helper was also a white supremacist, and not incidentally. He opposed slavery because of what it did to poor white southerners, not because of what it did to enslaved people. After emancipation he spent years campaigning for the expulsion of Black Americans from the country altogether, in books whose stated purpose was to "write the negro out of America". The anti-slavery argument and the racism were not in tension for him. They ran on the same engine.</p>
      <p>So here is Warning 2 above, in its sharpest form. If you argue for something you genuinely believe in, and you reach for economics to carry the case, ask yourself honestly whether you would still believe it if the economics came out the other way. If the answer is yes, then the economics was never your real reason. Leaning on it anyway rests your case on something you were never actually relying on, and hands anyone who disagrees a way to beat you that has nothing to do with what you think. Have the integrity to make the real argument.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Economic Methodology</h2>

    <p>Positive economics is the attempt to describe, explain and predict. This is how it is done, step by step.</p>

    <h3>Logic</h3>
    <p><strong>Logic</strong> is from the Greek <em>logiki</em>, meaning reason. It is reasoning in steps, where each statement holds if the ones before it hold:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>When a price rises, a good becomes less attractive to buyers.</li>
      <li>The price of bubble tea has risen.</li>
      <li>So fewer bubble teas get bought.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Statement 3 is not true because it sounds sensible. It is true only if 1 and 2 are true. That is the catch worth remembering: logic guarantees the <em>link</em> between statements, never the truth of the one you started with. Sound reasoning from a false premise gets you a confident wrong answer.</p>
    <p>Economics is a social science, so it builds knowledge the way sciences do, using the scientific method. The scientific method runs on logic.</p>

    <h3>Hypotheses</h3>
    <p>A <strong>hypothesis</strong> is an educated guess about a cause and effect relationship, usually written as <em>if ... then ...</em>. Building one takes three moves:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Observe and ask.</strong> Grab rides in Ho Chi Minh City are busier some weeks than others. Why?</li>
      <li><strong>Pick your variables.</strong> A <strong>variable</strong> is anything that can take different values. Here: the fare per ride, and the number of rides taken per day.</li>
      <li><strong>State the guess.</strong> If the fare rises, then the number of rides taken per day will fall.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Notice what that sentence does. It names a cause (fare) and an effect (rides), and it makes a prediction, which means someone can go and check it.</p>

    <h3>Ceteris paribus</h3>
    <p>For that hypothesis to make sense, we also need <strong>assumptions</strong>: statements taken as true for the purpose of building the hypothesis, whether or not they are true right now. The most important assumption in this course has a Latin name.</p>

    <div class="callout key-term">
      <div class="callout-title">Ceteris paribus</div>
      <p>Latin for "other things equal". When we say a relationship holds ceteris paribus, we are assuming that every other factor that could affect it is held constant, so that only the one factor we are interested in is allowed to move.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The point of doing this is <strong>isolation</strong>. Suppose a coffee shop in District 1 drops its prices, and sales rise the following week. Did sales rise because of the price cut? Maybe. But in that same week a new office block opened around the corner, the weather turned, and a competitor shut down. Four things moved at once, so the honest answer is that you cannot tell which one did the work, or how much of the effect belongs to each. The variables are tangled together.</p>
    <p>Ceteris paribus untangles them. By holding everything else still, we can say what the price change alone does, and we get a claim that can actually be tested. Without the assumption, almost no economic statement would be checkable, because in the real world the other variables never sit still long enough for you to look.</p>

    <div class="callout exam-tip">
      <div class="callout-title">Where you will meet this constantly</div>
      <p>Ceteris paribus is the reason we distinguish a <strong>movement along</strong> a curve from a <strong>shift</strong> of a curve. A change in price moves you along the curve, because everything else is being held constant. A change in anything else, income, tastes, input costs, is a change in one of the things we were holding constant, so the whole curve shifts instead. The same move appears in the PPC assumptions on 1.1, where we fix the quantity and quality of resources and the level of technology in order to say anything useful about the trade-off between two goods.</p>
    </div>

    <p>One warning, and it matters more than it first appears. Ceteris paribus does not claim that other things really are equal. It says nothing at all about the real world, where everything changes at once. It is a tool for building models, not a description of reality. That is why "does ceteris paribus actually hold here?" is a fair and often powerful thing to ask about any real-world case: if two things moved together, a prediction built on holding one of them still can quietly fail. You will see Friedman defend exactly this kind of unrealistic assumption in the history section below, on the grounds that a model needs to be useful rather than lifelike. Whether that defence satisfies you is a good question to sit with.</p>

    <h3>Empirical evidence</h3>
    <p><strong>Empirical evidence</strong> is real-world information: observations and data. The word comes from the Greek <em>empeiria</em>, meaning experience. Once you have a hypothesis, you go and check it against what actually happened.</p>
    <p>Here economics parts company with the natural sciences. A chemist can run the experiment again with one thing changed. An economist usually cannot, because you do not get to rerun 2020 with no lockdown. So economists work with data instead, using a branch of statistics called econometrics to ask whether the numbers fit the relationship claimed. Econometrics is university work and is not on the IB course, but the logic of it is: collect the fares and the ride counts, and see whether they move as predicted.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Data fit the prediction?</strong> The hypothesis survives, for now.</li>
      <li><strong>Data contradict it?</strong> Reject it and start again. If fares rose and rides rose too, our hypothesis is in trouble.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Refutation</h3>
    <p>To <strong>refute</strong> a claim is to disprove it. The requirement in any science is that refutation must be <em>possible</em>: there has to be some evidence that, if you found it, would show the claim to be false. This is also called falsifiability.</p>
    <div class="callout warning">
      <div class="callout-title">The test worth applying to everything</div>
      <p>If no possible evidence could prove a statement wrong, it is not a scientific statement. It might still be interesting, or true, or deeply held. It is just not doing the job we are asking economics to do here. Our Grab hypothesis passes: fares rising alongside rides rising would kill it.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Hypothesis, theory, law, model</h3>
    <p>These four get used loosely in conversation. They are not the same thing.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Term</th><th>What it is</th><th>Example</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Hypothesis</strong></td>
          <td>An educated guess about one cause and effect relationship. Testable.</td>
          <td>If the fare rises, rides fall.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Theory</strong></td>
          <td>A general explanation of a set of related events, built from hypotheses that have survived testing. Explains <em>why</em>.</td>
          <td>Utility theory, which explains why buyers behave that way (HL, 2.4).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Law</strong></td>
          <td>A concise statement taken to hold at all times and places, tested many times over. Describes, but does not explain.</td>
          <td>The law of demand (2.1).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Model</strong></td>
          <td>A simplification used to show how variables relate. In economics, usually a diagram.</td>
          <td>The PPC. The circular flow of income.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The law and theory distinction is the one students lose marks on. A law tells you <em>what</em> happens, a theory tells you <em>why</em>. The law of demand says buyers buy less at higher prices. It does not say a word about why.</p>
    <p>Models split two ways too. Some illustrate a theory, and there the words "model" and "theory" get used interchangeably. Others are purely descriptive: the <a href="/ibecon/unit1/1-1/">PPC and the circular flow</a> describe scarcity, choice, opportunity cost and how decision-makers connect, without explaining anything about why people act as they do. Descriptive models are not the poor relation. They are how you see the shape of a problem before you argue about its causes.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">History of Economic Thought</h2>
    <p>Why bother with the history? Two reasons. It shows how models evolved out of their context and the values of the people writing them, and it is good practice at separating positive from normative thinking. Each school emerged because the thinking before it could not explain something that had just happened.</p>

    <h3>Long before modern economics: the early middle ages</h3>
    <p>Roads made economics. Little mobility meant little trade, and with little trade there was not much sense in talking about an economy at all. Almost all the factors of production were land and labour, and land was power.</p>

    <h3>1720: Newton and the South Sea Bubble</h3>
    <p>Britain's South Sea Company was granted a trading monopoly, and its shares sparked a speculative mania: people bought because the price had gone up yesterday, not because of anything the company was actually worth. Isaac Newton lost a fortune when it crashed.</p>
    <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid var(--blue); padding-left:1rem; margin:1rem 0; font-style:italic; color:#444;">
      "I can calculate the motions of heavenly bodies, but not the madness of people."
    </blockquote>
    <p>After the crash, monarchs and philosophers went looking for a rational alternative to both market chaos and royal whim. That search is where modern economics begins.</p>

    <h3>18th century: Adam Smith and laissez-faire</h3>
    <p>In <em>The Wealth of Nations</em> (1776), Smith argued that order can emerge without a central planner. The "invisible hand" is the idea that prices signal where labour and capital should flow. The butcher, the brewer and the baker serve you out of self-interest, not benevolence, and the result is still a system that feeds the town.</p>
    <p>But Smith was not entirely laissez-faire. Markets need competition and information to work:</p>
    <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid var(--blue); padding-left:1rem; margin:1rem 0; font-style:italic; color:#444;">
      "People of the same trade seldom meet together, even for merriment and diversion, but the conversation ends in a conspiracy against the public, or in some contrivance to raise prices."
    </blockquote>
    <p>That is the argument for competition policy to protect consumers. He also wrote <em>The Theory of Moral Sentiments</em> (1759), arguing that humans are naturally empathetic rather than simply greedy, which is worth remembering before anyone claims Smith thought people were purely selfish. He was also famously absent-minded: he talked to himself in the street and once walked into a tanning pit mid-lecture.</p>

    <h3>19th century: Utility before the margin</h3>
    <p>Bentham held that the moral worth of an action is measured by the happiness it creates, and his "hedonistic calculus" tried to literally add up units of pleasure and pain. Mill carried utilitarianism forward more carefully. The concept of utility is still used in economic models today. Meanwhile the classical economists held that value came from the cost of production, not from subjective enjoyment.</p>

    <h3>19th century: Marginal utility</h3>
    <p>Jevons, Menger and Walras solved the diamond and water paradox: water is essential and cheap, diamonds are useless and expensive. The answer is that value comes from <strong>marginal</strong> utility, not total utility. The next glass of water is worth little, the next diamond a lot. Marshall turned this into the demand curve we still draw today.</p>
    <p>Marginal thinking also explains why incentives backfire. The cobra effect: a bounty on dead cobras led people to farm cobras, so the bounty was scrapped, so the farmers released their now worthless snakes. At the margin, people respond to the incentive you actually created, not the one you meant.</p>

    <h3>19th century: Say's Law and classical macro</h3>
    <p>"Supply creates its own demand", in a world of flexible prices. Paying workers to build things is exactly what gives them the money to buy things, round and round, so a general glut (everything unsold at once) was thought impossible. If things go wrong for a while, resources get reallocated, new households are paid for those resources, and spending restarts the economy. The implication is a self-correcting economy with a limited macroeconomic role for government.</p>

    <h3>19th century: Marx</h3>
    <p>Marx argued that capitalism is not self-correcting, it is self-destructing. His labour theory of value held that profit is unpaid labour, so exploitation is built into the arithmetic rather than being an abuse of an otherwise fair system. Wages get squeezed until workers cannot afford what they themselves make, and crisis follows. His historical materialism held that the economy drives law and politics, with the direction set by class conflict. He wrote <em>Das Kapital</em> in the British Museum reading room, while being famously unable to manage his own household finances. Loved by his family, though perhaps not the easiest husband.</p>

    <h3>20th century: Keynes and macroeconomics</h3>
    <p>The Depression broke Say's Law: the economy stayed stuck rather than self-correcting, demand was deficient and unemployment was involuntary. Wages are "sticky" downward, so firms cut jobs rather than pay. Keynes argued for government as the spender of last resort, with the multiplier rippling an initial injection of spending through the economy into a larger final increase in income. On waiting for markets to fix themselves:</p>
    <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid var(--blue); padding-left:1rem; margin:1rem 0; font-style:italic; color:#444;">
      "In the long run, we are all dead."
    </blockquote>
    <p>He made a fortune trading currencies from his bed before breakfast, and was part of the Bloomsbury set alongside Virginia Woolf.</p>

    <h3>Late 20th century: Monetarist and new classical views</h3>
    <p>Friedman argued that inflation is always and everywhere a monetary phenomenon: control the money supply rather than spending. He also held that there is a natural rate of unemployment that policy cannot permanently reduce. Lucas added rational expectations: people see the stimulus coming and adjust their behaviour, which blunts it.</p>
    <p>Friedman also made a methodological claim that matters for how you read every model in this course: a model's assumptions do not need to be realistic, they only need to be useful, in the same way that "a ball falling in a vacuum" predicts real falling balls well enough. Chicago had its share of characters too, including Stigler's academic feuds and a series of detective novels whose hero, Henry Spearman, solves crimes using rational choice theory.</p>
    <p>There is a darker side worth stating plainly: some in this tradition were willing to see democracy overthrown in order to protect economic liberty, in theory and in reality, most notoriously in Chile.</p>

    <h3>Capitalism's other critics: Veblen and Schumpeter</h3>
    <p>Veblen pointed out that we do not buy a Rolex to tell the time. "Conspicuous consumption" is consumption that signals status, which is hard to square with a model of consumers rationally maximising utility from the good itself.</p>
    <p>Schumpeter argued that capitalism's real engine is not price competition but "creative destruction": new technology burns down old industries in order to fund new wealth. Buggy whips, then typewriters, then taxis giving way to Uber and Grab, and now whatever AI is about to do. His worry was that capitalism's own success might eventually turn people against it.</p>

    <h3>Late 20th and 21st century: New directions</h3>
    <p>Economics has expanded in many directions:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Data-driven empirical methods</li>
      <li>Behavioural economics: limits of rationality, biases, and nudge theory</li>
      <li>Information economics: asymmetric information and market failure</li>
      <li>Game theory and market design</li>
      <li>Inequality research and distributional focus</li>
      <li>Climate economics and the circular economy</li>
      <li>Randomised controlled trials (RCTs) in development economics</li>
      <li>Digital platforms, network effects, big data, and AI</li>
      <li>Post-2008 and pandemic policy rethinks, including MMT debates</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

